That's the safety point, not the danger point.  An important
distinction!

> So, what that means is, if you're wearing a +3 chain mail, and you read
> a blessed scroll of enchant armor, there is a 2/3 chance (!) that it
> will be evaporated.

+3 is safe to enchant.  And the resulting enchantment can be +5
In my experience it's somewhere around 50/50 whether you'll get
+4 or +5.  (I'm not spoiled enough to know the exact odds).

There is zero chance of evaporating +3 chain mail with a blessed
scroll of enchant armor.

> What the hardcore spoiled nethacker does in this 
> situation, is to DISENCHANT the armor to +2, and then read the blessed 
> scroll for a max of +5.

You want to disenchant from +4 to +3 and retry for a max of +5.

If you disenchant to +2, your odds of ending up with +5 go way down.

>>So how do the folks here get +7 armor? Could someone enlighten me?
> 
> Elven armor is easier to get to +7.

Enchanting from +5 is safe if the armor is elven.  And the result can
be +7.  I don't normally use elven armor (my loss, I'm sure) so
I don't have a feel for the odds.

> If you're reading YAAP's where
> people have +7 or greater regular armor, it's probably because the
> player got bored between the castle and waking up Rodney, and had 
> too much loot for their own good.  I wouldn't try it in the early game,
> because a piece of armor that's +3 is a lot more valuable than a piece
> of armor that's evaporated.  I'd be more inclined to use the extra
> scroll for fireproofing than enchantment.  OTOH, I do try to make
> everything +5, in the rare games where I get the opportunity.

I usually figure that a +3 completely rusted piece of armor is
as good or better than a +0 rustproof piece of armor.  So I will
generally hold off on erodeproofing until I've got all my "keeper"
pieces up to +3.  On the other hand, erodeproofing a completely
rusted piece is often as good as adding a guaranteed +3 and doesn't
need a blessed scroll.  So if I have been careless with rust monsters
or have a holy water shortage, I may end up with some early
erodeproofing.

Since you can obtain +7 elven armor safely, one approach is to make
+7 elven armor pieces then polymorph them, hoping for some more
useful +7 armor.

IIRC, a cornuthaum in the hands of a wizard also works for enchantment
to +7.

